ted as an explanatory factor for symptom variability across patients [Data: Entities (5488, 5497, 5476); Relationships (12213, 12218)].\\n\\nMechanistically, the network distinguishes two broad drivers of symptoms: (1) PRESSURE ON THE BRAIN from the tumor mass effect and (2) INVASION OF BRAIN TISSUE, which can interfere directly with functional neural networks. Both are explicitly linked to gliomas as symptom-producing processes [Data: Entities (5499, 5500, 5476); Relationships (12220, 12221)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Cognitive and communication symptoms form a prominent cluster, with confusion and speech difficulties explicitly highlighted\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The entities PROBLEMS WITH THINKING AND SPEECH and CONFUSION establish a symptom cluster that includes cognitive and communication impairments. Confusion is explicitly framed as a cognitive symptom involving difficulty with clear thinking and communication and is listed as an example under problems with thinking and speech [Data: Entities (5518, 4121); Relationships (12310)].\\n\\nSpeech difficulties are also explicitly included as an example within the same symptom grouping, reinforcing that communication impairment is a recognized clinical manifestation in the brain tumor context [Data: Entities (5526, 5518); Relationships (12312)]. Additionally, confusion is listed as a possible symptom associated with primary central nervous system lymphoma, indicating overlap in neurocognitive symptomatology across different CNS malignancies (even when tumor types differ) [Data: Entities (4121); Relationships (8552)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Brain tumors are linked to progressive functional impairment and neurobehavioral change, including severe identity impact\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Beyond discrete cognitive symptoms, BRAIN TUMOR is linked to broader functional decline: memory loss, slower thinking, and inability to do everyday activities are explicitly listed as possible manifestations/effects of impaired brain function in people with CNS tumors. This frames impact not only as neurologic signs but as loss of independence and daily functioning [Data: Entities (4094, 4152, 4151, 4155); Relationships (12256, 12257, 12259)].\\n\\nThe community also includes behavioral and identity-related consequences. CHANGES IN BEHAVIOR and LOSS OF SENSE OF SELF are listed as possible manifestations/effects, with the latter described as a severe impact on brain function and identity. Together, these relationships emphasize that the disease burden extends into personality/behavioral domains, not only cognition [Data: Entities (5510, 4156, 4094); Relationships (12260, 12261)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Molecular characteristics and biomarker-based integrated diagnosis are presented as essential for classification and personalization\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The network connects GLIOMA to MOLECULAR CHARACTERISTICS and emphasizes that each glioma has its own molecular features and behavior, making each person’s glioma “unique.” This positions molecular profiling as part of how clinicians understand prognosis and disease behavior rather than relying solely on histology or imaging [Data: Entities (5487, 5476); Relationships (12211)].\\n\\nAn example of integrated diagnosis appears through ASTROCYTOMA IDH-MUTANT GRADE 2 and the IDH GENE: the integrated label combines tumor cell type (astrocytoma), biomarker status (IDH-mutant), and grade (2). This shows how the community ties together histologic lineage and biomarker loci in contemporary naming and stratification [Data: Entities (5574, 5573, 5479); Relationships (12432, 12467)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"High-grade disease is highlighted via grade 4 glioma and glioblastoma, described as highly destructive and fast-growing\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Severity within the glioma category is represented by GRADE 4 GLIOMA and GLIOBLASTOMA. Grade 4 glioma is described as having extremely